.TH SARIF-KIT 1 "2026-08-04" "sarif-kit" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sarif-kit \- convert native scanner and linter output into SARIF 2.1.0
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBsarif-kit convert\fR (\fB\-\-tool\fR \fINAME\fR | \fB\-\-auto\fR) \fB\-i\fR \fIPATH\fR \fB\-o\fR \fIPATH\fR
                  [\fB\-\-src\-root\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-\-dep\-file\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-\-fail\-on\-findings\fR]
\fBsarif-kit validate\fR \fIPATH\fR
\fBsarif-kit merge\fR \fB\-o\fR \fIPATH\fR \fIINPUT\fR [\fIINPUT\fR ...]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Many scanners and linters cannot emit SARIF, the format GitHub Code Scanning
reads.
.B sarif-kit
takes the native output such a tool already produces and turns it into valid
SARIF 2.1.0, ready to hand to GitHub's
.I upload-sarif
action.
It converts existing output files; it never runs the underlying tools itself.
.PP
Every command also accepts
.B \-\-help
and prints a short usage summary.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS convert
Convert one tool's native output to SARIF.
Exactly one of
.B \-\-tool
and
.B \-\-auto
must be given.
.TP
.BI \-\-tool " NAME"
The tool that produced the input.
One of
.BR pip-audit ,
.BR yamllint ,
.BR codespell ,
.B platformio
or
.BR pylint .
.TP
.B \-\-auto
Detect the tool from the shape of the input instead of naming it.
Input that matches no known tool, or more than one, is an error.
.TP
.BI \-i " PATH" "\fR,\fP \-\-input " PATH
The native output to read.
Use \- for stdin.
.TP
.BI \-o " PATH" "\fR,\fP \-\-output " PATH
The SARIF file to write.
Use \- for stdout.
.TP
.BI \-\-src\-root " PATH"
The repository root.
Absolute paths in the input are rewritten relative to it, which is what makes
the file links in GitHub's alerts resolve.
.TP
.BI \-\-dep\-file " PATH"
The manifest the findings point at, for
.B pip-audit
only.
pip-audit's JSON names packages, not files, so the alerts need a file to attach
to (default requirements.txt).
.TP
.B \-\-fail\-on\-findings
Exit 1 when the converted input contained any findings.
The SARIF output is still written.
Useful when the conversion itself should gate a CI job.
.SS validate
Check a SARIF file against the vendored SARIF 2.1.0 schema.
Takes one positional
.IR PATH ;
\- reads stdin.
Exits 0 when the file is valid and 1 when it is not.
.SS merge
Combine several SARIF files into a single log.
The runs of every input are concatenated in the order the files are given, and
each run keeps its own tool and rules, so nothing has to be renumbered.
A file that already holds several runs contributes all of them.
GitHub accepts at most 20 runs per uploaded file.
Merging past that fails here, with a clear message, instead of later when the
upload is rejected.
Merge one file per tool.
GitHub tells analyses apart by tool and category and rejects any file whose runs
share one, so merging those is an error here too.
Two yamllint files belong in two uploads with a category each.
.TP
.BI \-o " PATH" "\fR,\fP \-\-output " PATH
The merged SARIF file to write.
Use \- for stdout.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Findings were present
.RB ( "convert \-\-fail\-on\-findings" )
or the file failed schema validation
.RB ( validate ).
.TP
.B 2
Conversion, usage or IO error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Audit dependencies with pip-audit and convert the result.
The || true keeps the shell going when pip-audit exits nonzero on findings:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pip-audit \-r requirements.txt \-f json > pip\-audit.json || true
sarif-kit convert \-\-tool pip-audit \-i pip\-audit.json \-o pip\-audit.sarif \-\-dep\-file requirements.txt
.RE
.fi
.PP
Lint YAML.
yamllint exits 1 on errors, and the guard tolerates exactly that:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yamllint \-f parsable . > yamllint.txt || [ $? \-eq 1 ]
sarif-kit convert \-\-tool yamllint \-i yamllint.txt \-o yamllint.sarif
.RE
.fi
.PP
Spell-check the tree.
codespell exits 65 on typos:
.PP
.nf
.RS
codespell > codespell.txt || [ $? \-eq 65 ]
sarif-kit convert \-\-tool codespell \-i codespell.txt \-o codespell.sarif
.RE
.fi
.PP
Statically analyze a PlatformIO project.
pio check exits 0 even with findings, so no guard is needed, but its paths are
absolute and need \-\-src\-root:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pio check \-\-json\-output > pio\-check.json
sarif-kit convert \-\-tool platformio \-i pio\-check.json \-o pio\-check.sarif \-\-src\-root .
.RE
.fi
.PP
Lint Python.
pylint encodes what it found in its exit code, and \-\-exit\-zero clears that
without hiding a usage error, which still exits 32:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pylint \-\-exit\-zero \-\-output\-format=json2 src > pylint.json
sarif-kit convert \-\-tool pylint \-i pylint.json \-o pylint.sarif
.RE
.fi
.PP
Let sarif-kit work out the tool, validate the result, then merge several logs
into one:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sarif-kit convert \-\-auto \-i yamllint.txt \-o yamllint.sarif
sarif-kit validate yamllint.sarif
sarif-kit merge \-o combined.sarif pip\-audit.sarif yamllint.sarif codespell.sarif
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The upload half is GitHub's own
.I github/codeql-action/upload-sarif
action; sarif-kit deliberately stops at producing the file it accepts.
.PP
Per-tool capture commands, severity mappings and full CI workflow examples live
in the docs directory of the repository:
.I https://github.com/sarif-kit/sarif-kit
